---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';
import BoneFiltrationDemo from '../../components/BoneFiltrationDemo.astro';
---

<Layout
  title="Topology and Bone Fragility"
  description="An interactive research interface introducing John Rick Manzanares's work on topological and morphometric descriptions of trabecular bone microstructure."
>
  <header class="research-hero">
    <div class="shell research-hero-grid">
      <div>
        <p class="kicker">Research interface · Doctoral work</p>
        <h1>Can the shape of bone tell us something about its strength?</h1>
        <p class="research-lead">
          Trabecular bone is not simply “more bone” or “less bone.” It is a three-dimensional
          network whose connectivity, tunnels, cavities, orientation, and local geometry all
          contribute to its architecture.
        </p>
        <div class="actions">
          <a class="button" href="#explore">Explore the idea</a>
          <a class="button secondary" href="#pipeline">See the research pipeline</a>
        </div>
      </div>

      <aside class="research-question" aria-label="Research question">
        <p class="eyebrow">Central question</p>
        <p>
          Can topological descriptors capture aspects of trabecular architecture that complement
          conventional morphometry when modelling apparent bone strength?
        </p>
        <div class="question-chain" aria-label="Research chain">
          <span>micro-CT</span>
          <span aria-hidden="true">→</span>
          <span>structure</span>
          <span aria-hidden="true">→</span>
          <span>topology</span>
          <span aria-hidden="true">→</span>
          <span>strength</span>
        </div>
      </aside>
    </div>
  </header>

  <section class="section" id="explore">
    <div class="shell">
      <div class="section-heading">
        <div>
          <p class="kicker">Start with the idea</p>
          <h2>What topology adds</h2>
        </div>
        <p>
          Conventional morphometry measures quantities such as bone volume fraction, trabecular
          thickness, separation, connectivity density, and anisotropy. Topology asks a different
          question: what kinds of connected structures, loops, and enclosed voids exist, and how
          persistent are they as the image is filtered?
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="dimension-grid">
        <article>
          <span class="dimension">H<sub>0</sub></span>
          <h3>Components</h3>
          <p>Tracks when separate pieces appear and when they merge into larger connected structures.</p>
        </article>
        <article>
          <span class="dimension">H<sub>1</sub></span>
          <h3>Tunnels</h3>
          <p>Captures loop-like structure: paths that surround a passage without filling it in.</p>
        </article>
        <article>
          <span class="dimension">H<sub>2</sub></span>
          <h3>Cavities</h3>
          <p>Captures enclosed three-dimensional voids bounded by the surrounding structure.</p>
        </article>
      </div>

      <BoneFiltrationDemo />
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section depth-section">
    <div class="shell">
      <div class="section-heading">
        <div>
          <p class="kicker">Choose your depth</p>
          <h2>One project, three ways in</h2>
        </div>
        <p>
          The same research can be approached through intuition, mathematical structure, or implementation.
          The buttons below change the level of detail without changing the underlying scientific question.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="depth-interface" data-depth-interface>
        <div class="depth-tabs" role="tablist" aria-label="Explanation depth">
          <button type="button" role="tab" aria-selected="true" data-depth-tab="intuition">Intuition</button>
          <button type="button" role="tab" aria-selected="false" data-depth-tab="mathematics">Mathematics</button>
          <button type="button" role="tab" aria-selected="false" data-depth-tab="implementation">Implementation</button>
        </div>

        <div class="depth-panels">
          <section data-depth-panel="intuition">
            <p class="eyebrow">Intuition</p>
            <h3>A single threshold can hide structure.</h3>
            <p>
              A segmented image gives one binary answer: bone or not bone. Persistent homology instead
              studies a sequence of related shapes. Features that appear only briefly may reflect small-scale
              detail or noise; features that persist across a wider part of the filtration represent structure
              that survives a larger change in threshold.
            </p>
            <p>
              The goal is not to replace morphometry. It is to ask whether a different mathematical summary
              carries complementary information about architecture.
            </p>
          </section>

          <section data-depth-panel="mathematics">
            <p class="eyebrow">Mathematics</p>
            <h3>A nested family of cubical complexes.</h3>
            <p>
              Let <em>f</em> assign a scalar value to each cell of a cubical complex. For a threshold
              <em>t</em>, a sublevel filtration contains all cells whose filtration value is at most
              <em>t</em>:
            </p>

            <div class="math-display" aria-label="K sub t equals the set of cells Q such that f of Q is less than or equal to t">
              <math display="block">
                <msub><mi>K</mi><mi>t</mi></msub>
                <mo>=</mo>
                <mo>{</mo>
                <mi>Q</mi>
                <mo>:</mo>
                <mi>f</mi><mo>(</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo>)</mo>
                <mo>≤</mo>
                <mi>t</mi>
                <mo>}</mo>
                <mo>.</mo>
              </math>
            </div>

            <p>
              Whenever <em>s</em> is at most <em>t</em>, inclusion from
              <em>K</em><sub>s</sub> into <em>K</em><sub>t</sub> induces a map on homology:
            </p>

            <div class="math-display" aria-label="H k of K sub s maps to H k of K sub t">
              <math display="block">
                <msub><mi>H</mi><mi>k</mi></msub>
                <mo>(</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>)</mo>
                <mo>→</mo>
                <msub><mi>H</mi><mi>k</mi></msub>
                <mo>(</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>)</mo>
                <mo>.</mo>
              </math>
            </div>

            <p>
              Persistent homology records when homology classes are born and when they die. A persistence
              diagram summarizes a class by a point <span class="nowrap">(birth, death)</span>; its distance
              from the diagonal is related to how long the feature persists.
            </p>
          </section>

          <section data-depth-panel="implementation">
            <p class="eyebrow">Implementation</p>
            <h3>Images become scalar fields, then topological summaries.</h3>
            <p>
              The topological workflow is kept separate from the conventional morphometry executable.
              Three-dimensional image volumes are restricted to an analysis region, converted into scalar
              filtrations such as image intensity or signed-distance information, and processed as cubical
              complexes. Persistent homology is then vectorized for statistical modelling.
            </p>
            <p>
              This separation is intentional: the open-source <code>bone-morphometry</code> Rust program
              handles conventional and advanced non-persistent descriptors, while the persistent-homology
              workflow remains independently inspectable.
            </p>
            <div class="inline-links">
              <a href="https://github.com/jhnrckmnznrs/bone_morphometry" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">bone_morphometry →</a>
              <a href="https://github.com/jhnrckmnznrs/boneStrengthPrediction" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">topological workflow repository →</a>
            </div>
          </section>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section" id="pipeline">
    <div class="shell">
      <div class="section-heading">
        <div>
          <p class="kicker">Research provenance</p>
          <h2>From image to model</h2>
        </div>
        <p>
          A research interface should make the chain from data to conclusion visible. The current workflow
          is organized into explicit stages so that preprocessing, descriptors, and modelling can be inspected
          separately.
        </p>
      </div>

      <ol class="pipeline">
        <li>
          <span>01</span>
          <div>
            <h3>micro-CT volume and analysis region</h3>
            <p>Begin with a calibrated three-dimensional image and a defined region of interest.</p>
          </div>
        </li>
        <li>
          <span>02</span>
          <div>
            <h3>Preprocessing and segmentation</h3>
            <p>Construct a binary bone representation while keeping the analysis domain explicit.</p>
          </div>
        </li>
        <li>
          <span>03</span>
          <div>
            <h3>Scalar filtration</h3>
            <p>Use grayscale or distance-based scalar information to create a nested sequence of cubical complexes.</p>
          </div>
        </li>
        <li>
          <span>04</span>
          <div>
            <h3>Persistent homology</h3>
            <p>Track topological classes in dimensions 0, 1, and 2 across the filtration.</p>
          </div>
        </li>
        <li>
          <span>05</span>
          <div>
            <h3>Vector representation</h3>
            <p>Convert persistence information into representations suitable for comparison and statistical learning.</p>
          </div>
        </li>
        <li>
          <span>06</span>
          <div>
            <h3>Validation and structure–strength modelling</h3>
            <p>Compare topological and morphometric feature families using validation procedures appropriate for a small cohort.</p>
          </div>
        </li>
      </ol>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="shell evidence-grid">
      <article class="evidence-card">
        <p class="eyebrow">What is public now</p>
        <h2>Methods and software</h2>
        <p>
          The conceptual framework, computational workflow, and research software can be documented
          without publishing outcome tables prematurely. The Rust morphometry package is designed to
          expose descriptor families explicitly and includes validation material for independent checks.
        </p>
        <div class="inline-links">
          <a href="https://github.com/jhnrckmnznrs/bone_morphometry" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Inspect the Rust package →</a>
        </div>
      </article>

      <article class="evidence-card status-card">
        <p class="eyebrow">Research status</p>
        <h2>Results are still being prepared for publication.</h2>
        <p>
          Detailed strength-prediction comparisons and manuscript-level numerical results are intentionally
          omitted from this public interface for now. They can be added here once the corresponding work is public.
        </p>
      </article>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section next-questions">
    <div class="shell">
      <div class="section-heading">
        <div>
          <p class="kicker">What comes next</p>
          <h2>Questions beyond a single descriptor</h2>
        </div>
        <p>
          The project naturally leads to broader questions about directionality, invariance, local structure,
          mechanics, and learned field representations.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="question-grid">
        <article>
          <h3>Direction</h3>
          <p>How should topology be adapted when trabecular architecture is strongly anisotropic?</p>
        </article>
        <article>
          <h3>Invariance</h3>
          <p>Which descriptors remain meaningful when the coordinate frame changes?</p>
        </article>
        <article>
          <h3>Mechanics</h3>
          <p>How do topological changes relate to load paths, stiffness, and localized structural weakness?</p>
        </article>
        <article>
          <h3>Operator learning</h3>
          <p>Can topology help neural operators preserve structural information when predicting physical fields?</p>
        </article>
      </div>

      <div class="return-link">
        <a class="text-link" href="/research/">← Back to all research</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <script>
    const interfaceRoot = document.querySelector('[data-depth-interface]');

    if (interfaceRoot) {
      const tabs = Array.from(interfaceRoot.querySelectorAll('[data-depth-tab]'));
      const panels = Array.from(interfaceRoot.querySelectorAll('[data-depth-panel]'));

      interfaceRoot.dataset.enhanced = 'true';

      const select = (name) => {
        tabs.forEach((tab) => {
          const selected = tab.dataset.depthTab === name;
          tab.setAttribute('aria-selected', String(selected));
          tab.tabIndex = selected ? 0 : -1;
        });

        panels.forEach((panel) => {
          panel.hidden = panel.dataset.depthPanel !== name;
        });
      };

      tabs.forEach((tab, index) => {
        tab.addEventListener('click', () => select(tab.dataset.depthTab));

        tab.addEventListener('keydown', (event) => {
          if (!['ArrowLeft', 'ArrowRight'].includes(event.key)) return;
          event.preventDefault();
          const offset = event.key === 'ArrowRight' ? 1 : -1;
          const nextIndex = (index + offset + tabs.length) % tabs.length;
          tabs[nextIndex].focus();
          select(tabs[nextIndex].dataset.depthTab);
        });
      });

      select('intuition');
    }
  </script>

  <style>
    .research-hero {
      padding: clamp(4.5rem, 9vw, 8rem) 0 4rem;
    }

    .research-hero-grid {
      display: grid;
      grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1.15fr) minmax(320px, .85fr);
      gap: clamp(2rem, 6vw, 6rem);
      align-items: end;
    }

    .research-hero h1 {
      margin: 0;
      max-width: 14ch;
      font-family: ui-serif, Georgia, serif;
      font-size: clamp(3.2rem, 7.2vw, 6.6rem);
      font-weight: 500;
      line-height: .95;
      letter-spacing: -.052em;
    }

    .research-lead {
      max-width: 720px;
      margin: 1.5rem 0 0;
      color: var(--ink-soft);
      font-size: clamp(1.08rem, 1.7vw, 1.3rem);
    }

    .research-question {
      padding: 1.5rem;
      border: 1px solid var(--line);
      border-radius: 26px;
      background: rgba(255,255,255,.4);
      box-shadow: var(--shadow);
    }

    .research-question > p:not(.eyebrow) {
      margin: 0;
      font-family: ui-serif, Georgia, serif;
      font-size: 1.45rem;
      line-height: 1.35;
    }

    .question-chain {
      display: flex;
      flex-wrap: wrap;
      gap: .45rem;
      align-items: center;
      margin-top: 1.25rem;
      color: var(--ink-soft);
      font-size: .76rem;
      text-transform: uppercase;
      letter-spacing: .08em;
    }

    .question-chain span:nth-child(even) {
      color: var(--terracotta);
    }

    .dimension-grid,
    .question-grid {
      display: grid;
      grid-template-columns: repeat(3, minmax(0, 1fr));
      gap: 1rem;
    }

    .dimension-grid article,
    .question-grid article {
      padding: 1.4rem;
      border: 1px solid var(--line);
      border-radius: 20px;
      background: rgba(255,255,255,.35);
    }

    .dimension {
      color: var(--terracotta);
      font-family: ui-serif, Georgia, serif;
      font-size: 1.35rem;
    }

    .dimension-grid h3,
    .question-grid h3 {
      margin: .45rem 0 .5rem;
      font-family: ui-serif, Georgia, serif;
      font-size: 1.35rem;
    }

    .dimension-grid p,
    .question-grid p {
      margin: 0;
      color: var(--ink-soft);
    }

    .depth-section {
      background: rgba(70,96,82,.045);
    }

    .depth-interface {
      border: 1px solid var(--line);
      border-radius: 24px;
      overflow: hidden;
      background: rgba(255,255,255,.4);
    }

    .depth-tabs {
      display: flex;
      gap: .35rem;
      padding: .6rem;
      border-bottom: 1px solid var(--line);
      overflow-x: auto;
    }

    .depth-tabs button {
      appearance: none;
      border: 0;
      border-radius: 999px;
      padding: .65rem .9rem;
      color: var(--ink-soft);
      background: transparent;
      font: inherit;
      font-size: .88rem;
      font-weight: 750;
      cursor: pointer;
    }

    .depth-tabs button[aria-selected='true'] {
      color: var(--paper);
      background: var(--ink);
    }

    .depth-tabs button:focus-visible {
      outline: 3px solid var(--ochre);
      outline-offset: 2px;
    }

    .depth-panels {
      padding: clamp(1.4rem, 4vw, 2.5rem);
    }

    .depth-panels section {
      max-width: 800px;
    }

    .depth-panels h3 {
      margin: 0 0 1rem;
      font-family: ui-serif, Georgia, serif;
      font-size: clamp(1.8rem, 3vw, 2.7rem);
      line-height: 1.1;
    }

    .depth-panels p {
      color: var(--ink-soft);
    }

    .math-display {
      margin: 1.5rem 0;
      overflow-x: auto;
      padding: 1rem 1.25rem;
      border-left: 3px solid var(--terracotta);
      background: rgba(166,90,67,.06);
      font-size: 1.25rem;
    }

    .nowrap { white-space: nowrap; }

    .pipeline {
      position: relative;
      display: grid;
      gap: 0;
      margin: 0;
      padding: 0;
      list-style: none;
      counter-reset: pipeline;
    }

    .pipeline li {
      display: grid;
      grid-template-columns: 76px 1fr;
      gap: 1.2rem;
      padding: 1.35rem 0;
      border-top: 1px solid var(--line);
    }

    .pipeline li:last-child {
      border-bottom: 1px solid var(--line);
    }

    .pipeline > li > span {
      color: var(--terracotta);
      font-family: ui-serif, Georgia, serif;
      font-size: 1.35rem;
    }

    .pipeline h3 {
      margin: 0;
      font-family: ui-serif, Georgia, serif;
      font-size: 1.45rem;
    }

    .pipeline p {
      margin: .35rem 0 0;
      color: var(--ink-soft);
    }

    .evidence-grid {
      display: grid;
      grid-template-columns: repeat(2, minmax(0, 1fr));
      gap: 1rem;
    }

    .evidence-card {
      padding: clamp(1.4rem, 3vw, 2rem);
      border: 1px solid var(--line);
      border-radius: 22px;
      background: rgba(255,255,255,.4);
    }

    .evidence-card h2 {
      margin: 0;
      font-family: ui-serif, Georgia, serif;
      font-size: 2rem;
      line-height: 1.12;
    }

    .evidence-card > p:not(.eyebrow) {
      color: var(--ink-soft);
    }

    .status-card {
      border-color: rgba(197,164,93,.55);
      background: rgba(197,164,93,.08);
    }

    .question-grid {
      grid-template-columns: repeat(4, minmax(0, 1fr));
    }

    .return-link {
      margin-top: 2rem;
    }

    @media (max-width: 900px) {
      .research-hero-grid,
      .evidence-grid {
        grid-template-columns: 1fr;
      }

      .dimension-grid {
        grid-template-columns: 1fr;
      }

      .question-grid {
        grid-template-columns: repeat(2, minmax(0, 1fr));
      }
    }

    @media (max-width: 600px) {
      .research-hero h1 {
        font-size: clamp(3rem, 15vw, 4.7rem);
      }

      .question-grid {
        grid-template-columns: 1fr;
      }

      .pipeline li {
        grid-template-columns: 50px 1fr;
      }
    }
  </style>
</Layout>
